const content = "<p>A long-distance relationship is exclusive when both adults have clearly agreed to stop pursuing other romantic or sexual connections and understand what that agreement covers. Daily messages, affectionate names and plans to meet can show interest. They do not tell you whether the other person has stopped dating.</p>\n<p>If you have already stopped talking to other potential partners, the useful question is not whether your connection feels serious enough to count. It is whether you have both made the same choice. You can ask that without demanding a promise about marriage or pretending you already know how life together would work.</p>\n<p>We are building FilWest around serious Filipino-Western dating as we prepare for a private test. But serious intent is a starting point, not a relationship agreement. Neither an account nor a match can make that decision for you.</p>\n<h2>Ask When the Answer Starts Affecting Your Choices</h2>\n<p>There is no required number of calls or months before you can discuss exclusivity. Bring it up when the answer would change what you do: whether you accept another date, continue romantic conversations or invest more emotionally in this connection.</p>\n<p>You can also discuss it before meeting in person. Choosing to focus on one person does not mean you have resolved every question about compatibility. It means you are agreeing how to date while you find out. If either of you wants to meet first, say that plainly rather than allowing the other person to assume a commitment already exists.</p>\n<p>A direct opening could be:</p>\n<p>“I want to focus on this relationship and stop dating other people. Is that something you want too? For me, that would include not starting or continuing romantic or sexual conversations with anyone else.”</p>\n<p>That gives the other person a real proposal to consider. “Where is this going?” can open a useful discussion, but it may produce an answer about hopes rather than present behaviour. Someone can want a future with you while still believing you are both free to date.</p>\n<p>Choose a moment when neither person is rushing. If you use different first languages, explain the behaviour you mean instead of relying entirely on the word “exclusive.” Nationality does not establish anyone’s dating expectations. Ask the individual in front of you.</p>\n<h2>Make Room for an Honest Answer</h2>\n<p>A clear yes needs room for a genuine no. If the conversation becomes a test of love, the other person may feel pushed to agree before they are ready. You need an answer you can act on, not reassurance obtained through pressure.</p>\n<p>“I only talk to you” describes someone’s current activity. It does not necessarily mean they have agreed not to start another conversation tomorrow. Follow up gently: “Does that mean you want us both to date only each other from now on?”</p>\n<p>If the answer is “not yet,” clarify what that means for the present. Are you continuing to get acquainted without exclusivity? Would you both prefer to pause? You may decide you do not want to continue dating someone who is keeping other options open. That is a choice about your participation, not a reason to force their agreement.</p>\n<p>Avoid treating uncertainty as a promise with a delayed start. If you agree to revisit the question, choose a specific point to talk again. Until then, describe the relationship accurately rather than acting as though the hoped-for answer has already arrived.</p>\n<h2>Decide What Happens to Profiles and Other Conversations</h2>\n<p>Once you both agree, turn the label into practical decisions. Exclusivity becomes less ambiguous when you address the places where either person could still be presenting themselves as available.</p>\n<p>A genuine dating profile should represent the member’s current intentions accurately.</p>\n<p>Discuss whether each of you will stop browsing, stop accepting matches and stop responding to romantic approaches. Decide what you will do with existing profiles, using the options available on each service. Removing an app from a phone and changing the account itself are different actions, so “I deleted it” may need clarification.</p>\n<p>The point is not to perform a dramatic deletion on camera. It is to agree on conduct. A hidden profile would not resolve the issue if romantic conversations continued elsewhere, while an old visible profile needs an explanation rather than an immediate verdict.</p>\n<p>Existing conversations deserve attention too. If someone has been getting to know another potential partner, a brief, respectful closing message can prevent that person from being kept waiting. For example: “I’ve decided to pursue an exclusive relationship, so I won’t be continuing this as a dating conversation.”</p>\n<p>Ordinary friendships are a different matter. Do not turn “no other dating” into “no friends I feel threatened by.” If a particular friendship involves flirting or an unresolved romantic connection, discuss that behaviour specifically. A blanket ban on friendships avoids the actual question and restricts the other person’s independence.</p>\n<h2>Keep Privacy Separate From Secrecy</h2>\n<p>Exclusivity is a commitment about dating, not ownership of someone’s devices, accounts or time. You can expect honesty about conduct covered by your agreement without demanding access to every private conversation.</p>\n<p>Agree on what you may share publicly as well. One person may be ready to post a couple photo while the other wants to tell close friends privately first. Ask before posting photographs, screenshots or details from a personal conversation. Being together does not remove the need for consent.</p>\n<p>A useful privacy boundary might be: “I’m happy to talk about anything affecting our relationship, but I won’t share my passwords or other people’s private messages.” That leaves room for accountability without giving someone unrestricted account access.</p>\n<p>Likewise, an unanswered message is not evidence that exclusivity has ended. Work, sleep and time with other people do not stop because you are dating across distance. For expectations about calls and replies, our <a href=\"https://filipinawest.com/guides/long-distance-relationship-with-a-filipina\">long-distance relationship guide</a> addresses ongoing routines. Keep those arrangements separate from demands to prove where someone is throughout the day.</p>\n<p>The distinction becomes especially important when reassurance turns into surveillance. Compulsory location sharing, repeated demands for screenshots and pressure to cut off friends are not necessary steps toward commitment. The Australian eSafety Commissioner identifies tracking and isolation as behaviours that can form part of tech-based coercive control in its <a href=\"https://www.esafety.gov.au/key-topics/staying-safe/online-dating\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">eSafety online dating advice</a>.</p>\n<p>You have the right to be treated with respect and be safe when you are meeting people online.</p>\n<p>If saying no leads to threats, intimidation or pressure, focus on support and safety rather than negotiating more intrusive access. Talk to someone you trust. Where coercive control is involved, seek help with a safety plan before taking steps that could escalate the situation.</p>\n<h2>Separate Exclusivity From Bigger Commitments</h2>\n<p>Saying “I will date only you” does not also mean “I agree to move countries,” “I am ready to marry” or “I will send intimate images.” Those decisions require their own conversations and freely given agreement.</p>\n<p>This matters when the relationship is developing across different locations and personal circumstances. One person may see exclusivity as time to explore compatibility. The other may understand it as an immediate step toward marriage. Neither should have to discover that difference only after making expensive or life-changing plans.</p>\n<p>Ask what each of you believes comes next. Do you want the same kind of relationship? Are your expectations about children, family involvement or where to live compatible enough to keep exploring? Our <a href=\"https://filipinawest.com/guides/questions-before-a-filipino-western-relationship-gets-serious\">questions before the relationship gets serious</a> can help you start those conversations without bundling every future decision into one yes.</p>\n<p>Keep consent specific in the present, too. Agreement to be exclusive does not create an obligation to send photos, become sexual on a call or remain available whenever your partner wants attention. Affection and commitment do not make a reluctant answer into permission.</p>\n<h2>Set a Review Point, Not a Loyalty Test</h2>\n<p>Before ending the exclusivity conversation, agree when you will check how it is working. You might choose a date after you have had time to follow the new boundaries, or plan to talk after your first in-person meeting. The purpose is to make room for honest reflection, not to put the relationship on probation.</p>\n<p>A useful check-in asks whether the agreement still fits:</p>\n<p>“Are you still comfortable dating only each other? Did anything about profiles or other conversations remain unclear? Is there something either of us needs to discuss?”</p>\n<p>You can briefly confirm your initial agreement in a message if that helps, especially when language differences made the conversation difficult. Keep it mutual and ordinary: what you agreed, what each person will do and when you will talk again. It is a shared reminder, not material to use against someone later.</p>\n<p>A review date is not an automatic expiry date. Unless you agreed otherwise, do not assume you can resume dating other people because the check-in was postponed. If your intentions change, say so before acting outside the agreement.</p>\n<h2>Address a Concern Without Becoming an Investigator</h2>\n<p>If you notice something that appears inconsistent with your agreement, name the observation and ask about it. “Your profile now says you’re looking to meet someone. Can you explain that?” is more useful than an accusation based on a vague feeling.</p>\n<p>Separate what you know from what you suspect. A newly written invitation to date raises a different question from a profile you do not know how to interpret. You do not need to invent a harmless explanation, but you also do not need to treat every account detail as conclusive proof.</p>\n<p>Then consider the response alongside the conduct. Does the person address the specific concern? Can you agree on what needs to change? If you decide to continue, make the next action clear rather than relying on another broad promise.</p>\n<p>Avoid fake profiles, secret logins or recruiting friends to test loyalty. If maintaining the relationship seems to require constant investigation, consider whether you want to remain in it. You can decide an arrangement no longer works for you without collecting a complete case against the other person.</p>\n<p>The practical answer is a mutual, explicit agreement followed by behaviour that matches it. Ask whether you both want exclusivity, decide what happens to dating profiles and other romantic conversations, and choose a review point. Our guidance can help you prepare; the next step is a conversation with the person whose answer matters.</p>";
